    "turn_text": "political or policy-based at all. My parents were conservative and kind of a traditional and moral sense, but they were not politically active. And Arkansas in those days, really until just about eight years ago, it was a one-party democratic state. So the Democratic primaries, all that mattered. You know, my parents didn't put up yard signs or bumper stickers or anything because they didn't want to offend the other guys supporters because most politics in a one-party state is more based on like personality and geography and so forth, not ideas. So we talked more about the farm. You know, my parents talked about their work. My sister and I with my parents talked about school and our sporting or other extracurricular activities. What did your parents do? My father was an employee at the State Department of Health. He had started out after he got back from Vietnam and got an advanced degree in poultry science with a company that later got rolled up in Tyson's gradual growth in the industry. And that was, as a young man, probably mid-20s. They wanted him as part of their junior leader development program to move, I think, to Alabama. far away from our little hometown and he left that job because he didn't want to move. He had a father who was growing ill and to whom he was very close who had been widowed recently and that uncle is very close to so he decided to leave that job and go to work for the Department of Health could admit that although you know",
