    "turn_idx": 115,
    "turn_text": "So within political science, reading the great books of political philosophy, starting with Plato's Republic and going on down to Nietzsche and Heidegger, as well as American political thoughts, the thoughts surrounding our founding and Tocqueville and Lincoln and the Civil War and so forth. I tried to minimize the traditional modern political science I did. So you came to Harvard with this, with these conservative roots based on where you grew up and your parents. And in all of that, did you develop an intellectual framework for your service? So by the time I got to Harvard, I recognized that I was unusual in Arkansas, that I was a Republican. Bill Clinton's first year in office helped contribute to that. But again, I said I hadn't grown up in a political or partisan household. It's not like, you know, Now being a Republican politician and going to Republican events, you realize that there are people out there who are lifelong partisan Republicans. They're raised that way. They have 1,000 elephant scarves and pens and decorations at their house in the same way there are Democrats as well. I'm not having grown up like that though. I wasn't as reflexively partisan. as some people are. They grew up in that world out there, knocking on doors for candidates from the time they were five years old with their mom and dad. But I was a pretty conservative. And I would say at Harvard, I began to fill in more of the historical foundation",
