    "turn_text": "But also, you know, at the time to get physically prepared, go from just being a weekend warrior on the basketball court to someone, you know, who's physically prepared for the rigors of being an infantryman, going through infantry training, and then being on that. But you were offered, you were offered the opportunity to serve in the jazz, right? That was never crossed my mind. You said no. No, no, from the very beginning. I mean, like I said, I had a big shift in my mindset after the 9-11 packs. I no longer was thinking about the career progression I would take as a lawyer, you know, what I might end up doing. you know, in private practice, what I might end up doing as a prosecuting attorney or anything. I was very much thinking about going to the front lines and leading men in combat in defense of our country. So you get that in both Iraq and Afghanistan? Yeah. More so in Iraq than Afghanistan. You know, I was a platoon leader with the 101st Airborne. in Iraq. So that was every day going out on patrol Afghanistan. I was an operations officer for makeshift ad hoc unit called a provincial reconstruction team. So I was functionally the kind of the equivalent of a company commander because I had a lieutenant working for me and a platoon that he commanded. So he was on every day. I'd go on a patrol a couple of times a week with them. So I still had troop leading responsibilities, but I had a lot of more planning responsibilities for our commander as well. But that's really what I wanted to do. So",
