    "turn_text": "And he just laughed and said, I must not be a very good lawyer. But too, it's credit. He just signed me up. Offshore Cannon School contracts are the hardest contracts for a recruiter's sign, so oftentimes recruiters will, if you're like me, if you're a professional, direct you into that professional path, which is a much easier contract to sign, or just encourage you to be in a listed private from the very beginning. To his credit, he worked hard to get my OCS contract through. So two questions about that. One is I think the United States military in general and the United States Army in particular are remarkable teachers of leadership and remarkable creators of leaders. So what did you learn about leadership, particularly with men in combat? So first I agree there's no better Leadership training school in the United States military. I have slight bias for the infantry, but broadly speaking, Air Force Navy, Marine Corps, Army, no better training for leadership. And I'll tell all of your listeners what I tell individually or in small groups, you know, the high school students and the college students do my speak regularly. You should join the military. It doesn't matter who you are or what you do or what you think you're good at. You know, you can be, you know, a stand out three star stud in your high school. Or you can be the most kind of bookish introverted person. The military knows how to take you and to teach you how to be an effective soldier, sailor, airman, and marine. And it's still in you, not just practical skills, not just firing a rifle or running a supply chain. But intangible skills like leadership and teamwork and mission focus and discipline that will put you far ahead of your peers. So you know, especially when you talk to kids or getting out of college, you have a chance to go work at a prestigious, you know, investment bank or company like Google or Apple or Facebook and then go on to go to a fancy business school afterwards, maybe get startup money and start their own company. Those are all great things to do. But if you spend three years in your country's military, you can still do all those things. And I know at 22 of those three years, same like a very long time. But the way that your peers will see you, that your fellow citizens will see you, that future employers and potential investors will see you will be vastly different if you've spent that time serving your country in the military because they know what they're getting in this, in the same way that, you know, say Google or Facebook or Apple might recruit from Stanford or Caltech because they know that's certain, a branding or credentialing function employers across America know what they're getting when they're hiring a veteran. So no matter who you are, no matter what you want to do in life, I strongly encourage you to explore the United States military. And you know, the military for me was kind of an extension of a lot of the lessons I learned from my parents on the farm or from my coaches or in junior RTC about the one, the centrality of leadership. You know, the army doctrine leadership is the most dynamic element of combat power. One of the other ones, maneuver information, fires, and I forget the last one. But it's the most dynamic one. You saw that officer candidate school, 150 officer candidates.",
